"""Phase 2.1 web batch B: FastAPI, Node/Express-style APIs, REST design, TypeScript, security-aware coding."""
from gen21_common import *

B = Bank("programming", "p21wb")
d = B.d

# ---------------- FastAPI ----------------
d("fastapi", "Show me a minimal FastAPI endpoint with a path parameter and a query parameter.", "```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.get(\"/items/{item_id}\")\ndef read_item(item_id: int, verbose: bool = False):\n    return {\"id\": item_id, \"verbose\": verbose}\n```\n\n`item_id` appears in the path, so FastAPI treats it as a path parameter and validates it as an integer (a non-integer returns a 422 response). `verbose` is not in the path, so it becomes an optional query parameter: `/items/5?verbose=true`.", diff="easy")
d("fastapi", "How do I validate a JSON request body in FastAPI?", "Declare a Pydantic model and use it as the parameter type:\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nclass TaskIn(BaseModel):\n    title: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=120)\n    priority: int = Field(default=3, ge=1, le=5)\n\n@app.post(\"/tasks\", status_code=201)\ndef create_task(task: TaskIn):\n    return {\"title\": task.title, \"priority\": task.priority}\n```\n\nInvalid bodies are rejected automatically with a 422 response listing which fields failed. Keep separate input and output models so clients can't set fields like `id` or `is_admin`.", diff="medium")
d("fastapi", "What does Depends do in FastAPI?", "`Depends` declares a dependency: a callable FastAPI runs for you and whose result it passes into your endpoint. It's used for database sessions, authentication, pagination parameters and so on, and dependencies can have their own dependencies.\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\ndef pagination(skip: int = 0, limit: int = 20):\n    return {\"skip\": skip, \"limit\": min(limit, 100)}\n\n@app.get(\"/users\")\ndef list_users(page: dict = Depends(pagination)):\n    return page\n```\n\nIn tests you can override a dependency (`app.dependency_overrides[...]`) to inject a fake database.", diff="medium")
d("fastapi", "Should my FastAPI route be async def or def?", "Use `async def` when the function awaits non-blocking I/O (an async database driver, `httpx.AsyncClient`). Use plain `def` when it calls blocking code (a synchronous database library, `requests`, heavy file I/O): FastAPI runs those in a thread pool so they don't block the event loop. The worst combination is `async def` that calls blocking code, which freezes every request while it runs. If you must do CPU-heavy work, move it to a worker process.", diff="hard")
d("fastapi", "I get a CORS error calling my FastAPI backend from the browser. How do I fix it?\n\n`Access to fetch at 'http://localhost:8000/api' from origin 'http://localhost:5173' has been blocked by CORS policy`", "The browser blocks cross-origin requests unless the server says they're allowed. Add the CORS middleware with the exact origins you trust:\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom fastapi.middleware.cors import CORSMiddleware\n\napp = FastAPI()\napp.add_middleware(\n    CORSMiddleware,\n    allow_origins=[\"http://localhost:5173\"],\n    allow_methods=[\"GET\", \"POST\", \"PUT\", \"DELETE\"],\n    allow_headers=[\"Authorization\", \"Content-Type\"],\n    allow_credentials=True,\n)\n```\n\nDon't combine `allow_origins=[\"*\"]` with credentials; browsers reject it and it would be unsafe anyway. In development you can alternatively proxy API calls through your frontend dev server so the browser sees one origin. CORS is a browser protection, not authentication.", diff="medium")
d("fastapi", "Which HTTP status codes should my API return for create, validation errors, not found and unauthorized?", "Common choices:\n\n- 201 Created for a successful POST that creates a resource (with a `Location` header if you can).\n- 200 OK for reads and updates; 204 No Content for a successful delete with no body.\n- 400 for a malformed request; 422 for a well-formed request with invalid data (FastAPI's default for validation).\n- 401 when the caller isn't authenticated, 403 when they are but aren't allowed.\n- 404 when the resource doesn't exist (or you don't want to reveal that it does).\n- 409 for conflicts such as a duplicate unique value.\n- 429 when rate limited, and 5xx only for server faults.\n\nIn FastAPI: `raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail=\"Task not found\")`.", diff="easy")
d("fastapi", "How should I check a bearer token in FastAPI?", "Use a dependency so every protected route shares the same check:\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import Depends, HTTPException, status\nfrom fastapi.security import HTTPAuthorizationCredentials, HTTPBearer\n\nbearer = HTTPBearer()\n\ndef current_user(creds: HTTPAuthorizationCredentials = Depends(bearer)):\n    user = verify_token(creds.credentials)   # your function: check signature, expiry, audience\n    if user is None:\n        raise HTTPException(status.HTTP_401_UNAUTHORIZED, \"Invalid token\", headers={\"WWW-Authenticate\": \"Bearer\"})\n    return user\n```\n\nVerify the signature with a fixed expected algorithm, check expiry, issuer and audience, and keep the signing key in configuration, not in code. Authentication (who you are) is separate from authorisation (what you may touch), so also check ownership inside each route.", diff="hard")
d("fastapi", "My FastAPI endpoint returns the SQLAlchemy object with the password hash in it. How do I prevent that?", "Declare a response model that lists only the safe fields; FastAPI filters the output to it:\n\n```python\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict\n\nclass UserOut(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(from_attributes=True)\n    id: int\n    email: str\n\n@app.get(\"/users/{user_id}\", response_model=UserOut)\ndef get_user(user_id: int, db=Depends(get_db)):\n    return db.get(User, user_id)\n```\n\n`password_hash` isn't in `UserOut`, so it's never serialised. Having separate input, output and database models is a defence against accidentally exposing columns later.", diff="medium")
d("fastapi", "How do I test FastAPI endpoints?", "Use `TestClient` with pytest; it calls your app in-process without starting a server:\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi.testclient import TestClient\nfrom main import app\n\nclient = TestClient(app)\n\ndef test_create_task_validates_title():\n    r = client.post(\"/tasks\", json={\"title\": \"\"})\n    assert r.status_code == 422\n\ndef test_create_task():\n    r = client.post(\"/tasks\", json={\"title\": \"Write docs\"})\n    assert r.status_code == 201\n    assert r.json()[\"title\"] == \"Write docs\"\n```\n\nOverride dependencies (database, current user) in tests so they are fast and isolated.", diff="medium")
d("fastapi", "Review this FastAPI code:\n\n```python\n@app.get(\"/search\")\nasync def search(q: str):\n    rows = db.execute(f\"SELECT * FROM products WHERE name LIKE '%{q}%'\").fetchall()\n    return rows\n```", "Problems:\n\n1. **SQL injection**: `q` is formatted straight into the SQL. Use bound parameters.\n2. **Blocking call in `async def`**: a synchronous driver blocks the event loop; use `def` or an async driver.\n3. **Unbounded output**: no limit or pagination.\n4. **`SELECT *`** may expose columns you didn't intend.\n\n```python\nfrom sqlalchemy import text\n\n@app.get(\"/search\")\ndef search(q: str = Query(min_length=1, max_length=50), limit: int = Query(20, le=100)):\n    stmt = text(\"SELECT id, name, price FROM products WHERE name LIKE :pat LIMIT :lim\")\n    rows = db.execute(stmt, {\"pat\": f\"%{q}%\", \"lim\": limit}).mappings().all()\n    return rows\n```\n\n(`Query` comes from `fastapi`.) Escape `%` and `_` in `q` if literal matching matters.", diff="hard")
d("fastapi", "Where should I put business logic in a FastAPI app?", "Keep route functions thin: parse and validate the request, call a service function, translate the result into a response. Put the rules in plain Python modules (services) that don't import FastAPI, and put queries in a repository or data layer. That makes the logic easy to unit-test without HTTP and lets other entry points (CLI jobs, workers) reuse it.", diff="medium")
d("fastapi", "How do I run something after the response in FastAPI, like sending an email?", "Use `BackgroundTasks` for short, best-effort work:\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import BackgroundTasks, FastAPI\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\ndef send_welcome(email: str):\n    ...  # your email code\n\n@app.post(\"/signup\", status_code=201)\ndef signup(email: str, background: BackgroundTasks):\n    background.add_task(send_welcome, email)\n    return {\"status\": \"created\"}\n```\n\nThe task runs in the same process after the response is sent, so it is lost if the process dies. For work that must not be lost, or that is heavy or needs retries, use a proper job queue and worker.", diff="medium")
d("fastapi", "How do I load configuration (database URL, secret key) in a FastAPI project?", "Read settings from environment variables once at startup into a typed settings object, instead of hard-coding them:\n\n```python\nfrom pydantic_settings import BaseSettings\n\nclass Settings(BaseSettings):\n    database_url: str\n    secret_key: str\n    debug: bool = False\n\nsettings = Settings()\n```\n\n`pydantic-settings` is a separate package in Pydantic v2. Missing required values fail fast at startup. Keep real secrets out of the repository (use a `.env` file listed in `.gitignore`, or your platform's secret store), and never log them.", diff="medium")

# ---------------- Node / Express-style ----------------
d("node_api", "How do I handle errors in an Express app?", "Write a central error-handling middleware with four parameters, registered after your routes, and forward errors to it with `next(err)`:\n\n```javascript\napp.get('/users/:id', async (req, res, next) => {\n  try {\n    const user = await findUser(req.params.id);\n    if (!user) return res.status(404).json({ error: 'not_found' });\n    res.json(user);\n  } catch (err) {\n    next(err);\n  }\n});\n\napp.use((err, req, res, next) => {\n  console.error(err);\n  res.status(500).json({ error: 'internal_error' });\n});\n```\n\nIn Express 4, errors thrown in async handlers aren't caught automatically, hence the `try/catch` (Express 5 handles rejected promises for you). Don't send stack traces to clients.", diff="medium")
d("node_api", "What is middleware in Express?", "Middleware is a function `(req, res, next)` that runs in order for matching requests. It can read or change the request and response, end the response, or call `next()` to pass control on. Typical uses: logging, JSON body parsing (`express.json()`), authentication, rate limiting and error handling. Order matters: a middleware that needs a parsed body must be registered after the parser.", diff="easy")
d("node_api", "Why does `await` in my Express route not stop the server from serving other requests?", "Node runs your JavaScript on a single thread, but `await` on I/O (database, network, file) yields control, so other requests are handled meanwhile. What does block everyone is long synchronous work: `JSON.parse` on huge inputs, `fs.readFileSync`, crypto in a tight loop, a big `for` loop. Move CPU-heavy work to a worker thread or another process, and prefer the async versions of APIs.", diff="hard")
d("node_api", "How do I validate request input in a Node API?", "Never trust `req.body`, `req.query` or `req.params`. Validate shape, types and ranges at the edge with a schema library (such as Zod or Joi) and reject bad input with 400/422 before it reaches business logic:\n\n```javascript\nconst { z } = require('zod');\nconst Task = z.object({ title: z.string().min(1).max(120), priority: z.number().int().min(1).max(5).default(3) });\n\napp.post('/tasks', (req, res) => {\n  const parsed = Task.safeParse(req.body);\n  if (!parsed.success) return res.status(422).json({ error: 'invalid', details: parsed.error.issues });\n  res.status(201).json(parsed.data);\n});\n```\n\nValidation also strips unexpected fields, which helps prevent mass-assignment bugs.", diff="medium")
d("node_api", "How should I store a user's password in a Node app?", "Never store plain text or fast hashes like SHA-256. Use a slow, salted password hashing algorithm such as bcrypt or argon2 through a maintained library:\n\n```javascript\nconst bcrypt = require('bcrypt');\nconst hash = await bcrypt.hash(password, 12);        // store this\nconst ok = await bcrypt.compare(attempt, hash);       // on login\n```\n\nThe library generates the salt and embeds it in the hash. Enforce a sensible minimum length, rate-limit login attempts, and respond with the same message for unknown user and wrong password so you don't leak which accounts exist.", diff="medium")
d("node_api", "What's the best way to structure a Node API project?", "A common, practical layout separates concerns into layers: routes (HTTP only), controllers or handlers (parse input, call services, shape the response), services (business rules), and data access (queries). Configuration is loaded once from the environment, and the app creation is separate from `listen()` so tests can import the app without starting a server. Keep modules small and avoid circular imports. The exact folder names matter less than keeping HTTP details out of business logic.", diff="medium")
d("node_api", "Is it okay to use `process.env.SECRET` directly throughout the code?", "It works, but scattering it makes misconfiguration hard to catch. Read and validate all environment variables in one config module at startup, and fail fast if one is missing:\n\n```javascript\nfunction required(name) {\n  const v = process.env[name];\n  if (!v) throw new Error(`Missing required env var ${name}`);\n  return v;\n}\nmodule.exports = { jwtSecret: required('JWT_SECRET'), port: Number(process.env.PORT ?? 3000) };\n```\n\nNever commit `.env` files or log their values.", diff="easy")
d("node_api", "My Node server crashes with 'Unhandled promise rejection'. What does it mean?", "A promise was rejected (an async function threw, or `reject` was called) and nothing handled it with `catch` or `try/catch`. Since Node 15 this terminates the process by default. Fix the root cause: add `try/catch` around awaited calls, or `.catch()`, and make sure every promise is either awaited or has a handler. As a last-resort safety net you can log in `process.on('unhandledRejection', ...)`, but a crashing process after a logged error and a restart by a supervisor is usually safer than limping on in an unknown state.", diff="medium")
d("node_api", "How do I rate limit an API?", "Count requests per client (IP, API key or user) in a time window and respond with 429 and a `Retry-After` header when the limit is exceeded. In Express, middleware such as `express-rate-limit` does this for a single instance. If you run multiple instances, the counters must live in shared storage (like Redis), or a gateway or CDN must enforce the limit. Apply stricter limits to sensitive endpoints such as login and password reset.", diff="medium")

# ---------------- REST design ----------------
d("rest_design", "How should I design pagination for a list endpoint?", "Two common patterns:\n\n- **Offset/limit** (`?page=3&per_page=20`): simple and lets users jump to a page, but slow on large offsets and unstable if rows are inserted between requests.\n- **Cursor/keyset** (`?after=<last_id>&limit=20`): the server returns the next cursor; it stays fast on large tables and is stable under inserts.\n\nWhichever you choose, cap the maximum page size, use a deterministic sort order (add a tie-breaker such as `id`), and return pagination info:\n\n```json\n{\"items\": [], \"next_cursor\": \"eyJpZCI6IDQyfQ\"}\n```", diff="medium")
d("rest_design", "What does idempotent mean for an API, and which methods should be?", "An operation is idempotent if repeating it has the same effect as doing it once. `GET`, `PUT`, `DELETE` are meant to be idempotent; `POST` is not by default, so a retried request can create duplicates. For operations like payments, accept an `Idempotency-Key` header: store the key with the result and, if the same key arrives again, return the saved response instead of repeating the work. This makes client retries after timeouts safe.", diff="hard")
d("rest_design", "What should an API error response look like?", "Be consistent and machine-readable: a status code that matches the problem, a stable error code clients can switch on, a human message, and field details for validation errors.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"error\": {\n    \"code\": \"validation_failed\",\n    \"message\": \"One or more fields are invalid.\",\n    \"fields\": [{\"field\": \"email\", \"issue\": \"must be a valid address\"}]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nDon't leak stack traces, SQL or internal paths. Some teams adopt the standard Problem Details format (RFC 9457) for the same purpose.", diff="medium")
d("rest_design", "How should I version my API?", "Pick one scheme and apply it consistently: a path prefix (`/v1/users`) is the simplest and most visible; headers or media types are cleaner in theory but harder to use and cache. Only bump the version for breaking changes; adding optional fields or new endpoints is backward compatible. Announce deprecations with a date and a `Deprecation`/`Sunset` header where you can, and keep the old version running until clients have migrated.", diff="medium")
d("rest_design", "PUT or PATCH for updating a resource?", "`PUT` replaces the whole resource with the representation you send (missing fields are cleared or defaulted), and is idempotent. `PATCH` applies a partial change (only the fields sent). For most UI-driven updates, `PATCH` matches what clients want. Whatever you choose, validate which fields may be changed, and never let clients update fields like `id`, `role` or `owner_id` by passing them in the body.", diff="medium")
d("rest_design", "Should I use plural or singular nouns in REST URLs, and what about actions?", "Use plural nouns for collections (`/orders`, `/orders/42`) and let the HTTP method express the action: `GET /orders`, `POST /orders`, `DELETE /orders/42`. For operations that aren't plain CRUD, either model them as a sub-resource (`POST /orders/42/cancellation`) or a clearly named action endpoint (`POST /orders/42/cancel`). Consistency matters more than purity.", diff="easy")
d("rest_design", "How do I make an endpoint safe for concurrent edits (two users editing the same record)?", "Use optimistic concurrency. Include a version number or `ETag` with each record; the client sends it back with its update (`If-Match` header or a `version` field). The server applies the update only if the version still matches, using something like `UPDATE ... SET ..., version = version + 1 WHERE id = :id AND version = :version`. If no row was updated, return 409 (or 412) so the client can reload and merge. Pessimistic locks are an alternative for short critical sections but hurt throughput.", diff="hard")
d("rest_design", "When would GraphQL make sense instead of REST?", "GraphQL fits when many different clients need different shapes of the same data (a mobile app wants less than a web app) and you want to avoid over-fetching and many round trips. The costs: query complexity and N+1 problems need careful control, caching is harder than with plain HTTP, and authorisation must be enforced per field. For a simple CRUD API with one client, REST is usually simpler.", diff="hard")
d("rest_design", "What's a good way to handle file uploads in an API?", "Validate size, type and count on the server, never trusting the client's file name or `Content-Type`. Store files outside the web root or in object storage with generated names, serve them with a safe `Content-Type` and `Content-Disposition`, and scan if the content is risky. For large files, let clients upload directly to object storage with a short-lived signed URL so your API server isn't a bottleneck.", diff="medium")

# ---------------- TypeScript ----------------
d("typescript", "What's the difference between `interface` and `type` in TypeScript?", "For object shapes they are largely interchangeable. `interface` can be extended and merged (declaration merging: two interfaces with the same name combine), which suits public library APIs. `type` can also name unions, intersections, tuples and mapped types, which an interface can't do. A practical rule: use `interface` for object contracts, `type` for everything else, and be consistent within a codebase.", diff="medium")
d("typescript", "TypeScript says 'Object is possibly undefined' on this line:\n\n```typescript\nconst el = document.getElementById('app');\nel.innerHTML = 'hi';\n```", "`getElementById` returns `HTMLElement | null`, because the element may not exist, and with `strictNullChecks` you must handle that. Prefer a check over a non-null assertion:\n\n```typescript\nconst el = document.getElementById('app');\nif (!el) throw new Error('#app not found');\nel.innerHTML = 'hi';\n```\n\n`el!.innerHTML` silences the error but will throw at runtime if the element is missing.", diff="easy")
d("typescript", "What does `as` do, and when is it a bad idea?", "`as` is a type assertion: you tell the compiler to treat a value as a given type. It does no runtime check. It's reasonable when you know more than the compiler (for example, narrowing a DOM element to `HTMLInputElement`). It's dangerous for external data such as `JSON.parse(x) as User`, where nothing guarantees the shape. Validate the data (hand-written guard or a schema library) and let the validator produce the type instead.", diff="medium")
d("typescript", "Explain generics with a practical example.", "Generics let one function or type work over many types while keeping them connected.\n\n```typescript\nfunction groupBy<T, K extends string | number>(items: T[], key: (item: T) => K): Record<K, T[]> {\n  const out = {} as Record<K, T[]>;\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const k = key(item);\n    (out[k] ??= []).push(item);\n  }\n  return out;\n}\n\nconst byDept = groupBy([{ name: 'Ana', dept: 'eng' }, { name: 'Bo', dept: 'ops' }], (p) => p.dept);\n```\n\n`T` is inferred as the element type, so `byDept.eng` is typed as an array of those objects, with no `any`.", diff="hard")
d("typescript", "Utility types - which ones should I know?", "The most useful: `Partial<T>` (all fields optional), `Required<T>`, `Readonly<T>`, `Pick<T, 'a' | 'b'>` (keep some fields), `Omit<T, 'password'>` (drop fields), `Record<K, V>` (object with given key and value types), `ReturnType<typeof fn>`, and `NonNullable<T>`. Example: `type PublicUser = Omit<User, 'passwordHash'>;` keeps the types in sync as `User` evolves.", diff="medium")
d("typescript", "How do I type a function that can return different shapes depending on input?", "Use overloads, or a discriminated union for the result.\n\n```typescript\ntype Result<T> = { ok: true; value: T } | { ok: false; error: string };\n\nfunction parsePort(s: string): Result<number> {\n  const n = Number(s);\n  return Number.isInteger(n) && n > 0 && n < 65536 ? { ok: true, value: n } : { ok: false, error: 'invalid port' };\n}\n\nconst r = parsePort('8080');\nif (r.ok) console.log(r.value); else console.error(r.error);\n```\n\nThe `ok` field lets the compiler narrow the type in each branch, so callers can't read `value` without checking.", diff="hard")
d("typescript", "Why do I get 'Type string is not assignable to type \"a\" | \"b\"'?", "A string literal union only accepts those exact literals. A variable declared with `let x = 'a'` is widened to `string`, which doesn't fit. Fix it by annotating the type (`let x: 'a' | 'b' = 'a'`), using `const x = 'a'` (which keeps the literal type), or `as const` on objects: `const opts = { mode: 'a' } as const;`.", diff="medium")
d("typescript", "Should I turn on `strict` in tsconfig for an existing project?", "Yes, ideally, but migrate gradually. `strict` enables `strictNullChecks`, `noImplicitAny` and several related checks that catch real bugs. On a large codebase, turn the flags on one at a time (or per-folder with project references), fix the errors in each step, and avoid blanket `any` or `@ts-ignore` to make the errors go away. New code can be strict from day one.", diff="medium")

# ---------------- security-aware ----------------
d("security", "What is XSS and how do I prevent it?", "Cross-site scripting happens when an attacker's text ends up in your page as executable HTML or script, for example a comment containing `<script>`. Prevent it by escaping output for the context it's placed in (modern frameworks do this by default for text), avoiding `innerHTML`/`dangerouslySetInnerHTML` with untrusted data, sanitising any HTML you must allow with a vetted library, and adding a Content-Security-Policy. Also mark session cookies `HttpOnly` so injected script can't read them.", diff="medium")
d("security", "What's CSRF and do I need protection if I use JWTs?", "CSRF tricks a logged-in user's browser into sending a request to your site, relying on cookies being attached automatically. If authentication is by a cookie (session or JWT in a cookie), you need protection: `SameSite=Lax` or `Strict` cookies, plus CSRF tokens for state-changing requests where appropriate. If the token is sent explicitly in an `Authorization` header by your own JavaScript, browsers don't attach it automatically, so classic CSRF doesn't apply, but then the token is reachable by script, which makes XSS more serious.", diff="hard")
d("security", "Is it safe to store a JWT in localStorage?", "It's a trade-off. Anything in `localStorage` can be read by any script running on the page, so an XSS bug lets an attacker steal the token. An `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite` cookie can't be read by script, but then you need CSRF defences. Many teams use short-lived access tokens kept in memory plus a refresh token in an HttpOnly cookie. Whatever you pick, keep tokens short-lived, and make XSS prevention a priority.", diff="hard")
d("security", "What are common JWT mistakes?", "Typical ones: accepting the algorithm named in the token header (always specify the allowed algorithm on verification, and reject `none`); not checking expiry, issuer or audience; putting sensitive data in the payload (it is only encoded, not encrypted); using a weak or hard-coded signing secret; making tokens long-lived with no way to revoke them; and using a JWT where a simple server-side session would be easier and safer.", diff="hard")
d("security", "How do I prevent SQL injection in code?", "Never build SQL by concatenating or formatting user input. Use parameterised queries or your ORM's bound parameters, so the driver sends the SQL and the values separately:\n\n```python\ncur.execute(\"SELECT id FROM users WHERE email = %s\", (email,))\n```\n\nParameters protect values, not identifiers: for dynamic table or column names, check them against an allow-list. Also give the application's database account only the privileges it needs.", diff="easy")
d("security", "What's wrong with committing a .env file?", "It puts secrets (API keys, database passwords, signing keys) into version control history, where anyone with repo access, including future contributors and forks, can read them, and deleting the file later doesn't remove it from history. Add `.env` to `.gitignore`, commit a `.env.example` with placeholder names, and load real values from your host's secret store. If a secret was ever committed, treat it as compromised: rotate it.", diff="easy")
d("security", "How do I handle user-uploaded images safely?", "Check the actual content, not just the extension: verify the file type with a library, limit the size and dimensions, and re-encode images (which strips embedded payloads and metadata). Store them under random names outside the web root or in object storage, serve from a separate domain or with `Content-Disposition` and `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, and never execute or include uploaded files. SVG can contain script, so treat it as active content.", diff="hard")
d("security", "Review this login code for security issues:\n\n```python\n@app.post(\"/login\")\ndef login(email: str, password: str):\n    user = db.query(User).filter(User.email == email).first()\n    if user is None:\n        return {\"error\": \"No such user\"}\n    if user.password != password:\n        return {\"error\": \"Wrong password\"}\n    return {\"token\": str(user.id)}\n```", "Several serious problems:\n\n1. **Plain-text password comparison**: passwords must be stored as salted slow hashes (bcrypt, argon2) and verified with the library.\n2. **User enumeration**: different messages for unknown user and wrong password reveal which emails exist. Return one generic message.\n3. **The token is the user id**: anyone can guess it. Issue a random session token or a signed, expiring token.\n4. **Credentials as query parameters**: `email` and `password` declared like this come from the URL query string; they should be in the POST body over HTTPS, since URLs end up in logs.\n5. **No rate limiting or lockout**, so brute force is easy.\n\n```python\nGENERIC = {\"error\": \"Invalid email or password\"}\n\n@app.post(\"/login\")\ndef login(body: LoginIn):\n    user = get_user_by_email(body.email)\n    if user is None or not verify_password(body.password, user.password_hash):\n        raise HTTPException(401, GENERIC[\"error\"])\n    return {\"token\": create_session_token(user.id)}\n```", diff="hard")
d("security", "What is a Content-Security-Policy?", "A CSP is a response header that tells the browser which sources of scripts, styles, images and other resources are allowed. For example `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'` blocks inline scripts and scripts from other origins, which greatly limits what an XSS bug can do. Roll it out in report-only mode first to find what breaks, avoid `'unsafe-inline'`, and use nonces or hashes for scripts you must inline.", diff="hard")
d("security", "Should I validate input on the client, the server, or both?", "Both, for different reasons. Client-side validation is for user experience: quick feedback. It's not a security control, since anyone can bypass the browser and send raw requests. Server-side validation is mandatory: check types, ranges, lengths, allowed values and ownership every time. Share the rules (schemas) between client and server where your stack allows it, so they don't drift.", diff="easy")
d("security", "How do I store API keys in a frontend app?", "You can't keep a secret in code that ships to the browser; anyone can read it from the bundle or network tab. Keys that must stay secret belong on your server: the frontend calls your backend, which calls the third-party API. Public keys designed for client use (such as a map tile key) should be restricted by domain and quota in the provider's settings.", diff="medium")
d("security", "What does `HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax` on a cookie do?", "`HttpOnly` hides the cookie from JavaScript (limits theft via XSS). `Secure` sends it only over HTTPS. `SameSite=Lax` stops the browser from sending it on most cross-site requests (reducing CSRF) while still allowing top-level navigations such as clicking a link. A session cookie should normally have all three, plus a sensible expiry.", diff="medium")
d("security", "Explain the principle of least privilege in code and infrastructure.", "Give every user, service and process only the permissions it needs, for only as long as it needs them. For an app: a database account that can't drop tables, an API token scoped to read-only if it only reads, containers that don't run as root, and role checks on each endpoint. If something is compromised, the damage is limited to what that component could do.", diff="easy")

